//! Digital signals.

import std::sources::Rail

/// A digital line together with the rail that defines its logic levels. The line swings
/// between the rail's `neg` and `pos`.
///
/// The four levels are voltages relative to the rail's `neg`, as a part's datasheet gives
/// them, and a part states those that it has. A connection between two lines does not
/// equate them: each belongs to its own pin (ADR 0046).
///
/// - `vih`: the lowest voltage an input reads as high.
/// - `vil`: the highest voltage an input reads as low.
/// - `voh`: the lowest voltage an output drives a high to, at the load the datasheet names.
/// - `vol`: the highest voltage an output drives a low to, at the load the datasheet names.
///
/// After the rails are solved, the solver requires of each output on a net that its `voh`
/// is always at least the `vih` of every input there, and its `vol` at most their `vil`; it
/// says "not evaluated" when a level has no value or the two grounds are not the same net.
/// The pin `.line` has `.abs_min` and `.abs_max`, which the solver checks against the
/// worst-case voltage of its net in the same way.
interface Logic {
    .line: Pin
    .reference: Rail
    .vih: Voltage
    .vil: Voltage
    .voh: Voltage
    .vol: Voltage
}

/// The logic levels of all the lines of a bus interface, as a datasheet gives them for a
/// supply: the four voltages of `Logic` and the rail they are relative to. A bus interface
/// (`std::bus::Spi`, `Uart`, `I2c`, `Swd`) has one as `.levels`, so a part that has a bus states
/// them once for the interface: `.spi.levels.reference ~ .vdd`, `.spi.levels.vih &= 0.7 *
/// .vdd.voltage`, and an input's `.spi.sclk.abs_max &= .vdd.voltage + 0.3V`. The solver judges
/// a bus pin as it judges the `.line` of a `Logic`, with the numbers of ADR 0046; which lines
/// a part drives and which it reads is what its pin table says. `~` between two bus interfaces
/// joins no levels (each belongs to its own part, and the reference rail is that part's
/// supply), and a part that states none of them is not judged.
///
/// The reference rail is a reference for the checker and not a load: a part that joins it to
/// its supply (`.spi.levels.reference ~ .vdd`) gives the net a rail whose current nobody could
/// state, so `Levels` states it, `.reference.current &= 0A`, and the part's draw is all in its
/// supply's `current`.
interface Levels {
    .reference: Rail
    .reference.current &= 0A
    .vih: Voltage
    .vil: Voltage
    .voh: Voltage
    .vol: Voltage
}
